1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:06,113
In 2014, North Korea reportedly banned something coming in from the South.

2
00:00:06,393 --> 00:00:10,948
Not a weapon. Not a phone. Not a K-drama on a USB stick.

3
00:00:11,228 --> 00:00:15,322
A snack. A small, round, chocolate-covered snack.

4
00:00:16,052 --> 00:00:22,443
It's called Choco Pie. Two soft cakes, marshmallow in the middle, wrapped in chocolate.

5
00:00:22,723 --> 00:00:28,181
In South Korea, it costs less than a cup of coffee. Every convenience store has it.

6
00:00:28,461 --> 00:00:33,153
So how does a snack this ordinary end up worrying a government?

7
00:00:33,883 --> 00:00:38,522
This little cake has lived a stranger life than most people.

8
00:00:38,802 --> 00:00:47,057
It was invented after a trip to America. It became a kind of currency for Korean soldiers. It conquered Russia,

9
00:00:47,057 --> 00:00:49,809
ended up on family altars in Vietnam,

10
00:00:50,089 --> 00:00:55,470
and at the end, it crossed into North Korea, where things got really strange.

11
00:00:55,750 --> 00:01:01,622
Stay to the end, because the last part involves balloons. Ten thousand Choco Pies,

12
00:01:01,622 --> 00:01:04,988
floating over the most guarded border on earth.

13
00:01:05,718 --> 00:01:10,430
Let's start at the beginning. And the beginning is not in Korea.

14
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:18,137
In 1973, a researcher from a Korean confectionery company was traveling in the American South.

15
00:01:18,417 --> 00:01:23,333
At a hotel in Georgia, he was served a chocolate-coated sweet with his coffee.

16
00:01:23,613 --> 00:01:30,001
One bite, and he had an idea. What if Korea had a snack like this, cheap enough for anyone?

17
00:01:30,731 --> 00:01:34,681
He brought the idea home, and the team started experimenting.

18
00:01:34,961 --> 00:01:40,517
The next year, in 1974, Choco Pie hit Korean shelves.

19
00:01:40,797 --> 00:01:46,237
Korea was still a poor country back then. A chocolate snack felt like a small luxury,

20
00:01:46,237 --> 00:01:49,886
something you'd save, or give to someone you cared about.

21
00:01:50,616 --> 00:01:56,677
And that's where Choco Pie found its secret weapon. One Korean word: jeong.

22
00:01:56,957 --> 00:02:05,261
Jeong is hard to translate. It's the warm, sticky feeling between people who've been through things together. Your grandma

23
00:02:05,261 --> 00:02:09,549
pushing food on you. A friend who shows up without being asked.

24
00:02:09,829 --> 00:02:17,116
Orion printed that word right on the box. Choco Pie wasn't just a snack anymore. It was how you said,

25
00:02:17,116 --> 00:02:19,713
I care about you, without saying it.

26
00:02:20,443 --> 00:02:25,378
Which brings us to the most unlikely fan base of all. Korean soldiers.

27
00:02:26,108 --> 00:02:32,747
In South Korea, almost every young man serves in the military for around a year and a half.

28
00:02:33,027 --> 00:02:38,061
Life on base is strict, food is plain, and sweets are rare.

29
00:02:38,341 --> 00:02:46,451
So a box of Choco Pies becomes something like money. You trade them. You share them. You owe someone a Choco Pie.

30
00:02:47,181 --> 00:02:50,760
There's a famous joke that every Korean man knows.

31
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:58,710
Why do so many soldiers suddenly become religious on Sunday? Because the services hand out free Choco Pies.

32
00:02:58,990 --> 00:03:06,052
Ask any Korean guy who served. Most of them will laugh, and then admit it's at least a little bit true.

33
00:03:06,782 --> 00:03:12,129
Now, a snack that powerful was never going to stay inside Korea.

34
00:03:12,859 --> 00:03:20,241
In the early nineteen-nineties, the Soviet Union had just collapsed. Shelves in Russia's far east were empty.

35
00:03:20,521 --> 00:03:28,590
Russian traders started sailing to Korean ports like Busan, filling huge bags with anything they could resell back home.

36
00:03:28,870 --> 00:03:33,662
And one of the things they kept buying, by the boxful, was Choco Pie.

37
00:03:34,392 --> 00:03:37,877
It turned out to be a perfect match for Russia.

38
00:03:38,157 --> 00:03:45,747
It doesn't need a fridge. It survives long train rides across Siberia. And it goes beautifully with a glass of hot tea,

39
00:03:45,747 --> 00:03:48,234
which is how many Russians end the day.

40
00:03:48,514 --> 00:03:53,756
By twenty sixteen, Russians were buying around six hundred million packs a year.

41
00:03:54,486 --> 00:04:00,884
Today Orion makes Choco Pies inside Russia, and the factories still can't keep up.

42
00:04:01,164 --> 00:04:09,396
The lines have been running above their normal capacity, so the company is building a whole new factory building in the Tver

43
00:04:09,396 --> 00:04:13,712
region, northwest of Moscow, aiming to roughly double production.

44
00:04:13,992 --> 00:04:20,106
Think about that. A Korean snack is so popular in Russia that they're running out of room to make it.

45
00:04:20,836 --> 00:04:26,043
Next stop, Vietnam. And this is where Choco Pie got a promotion.

46
00:04:26,773 --> 00:04:33,709
In Vietnam, families honor their ancestors during the Lunar New Year with an altar full of offerings.

47
00:04:33,989 --> 00:04:39,649
Fruit, flowers, cakes. And in a lot of homes, a box of Choco Pie.

48
00:04:39,929 --> 00:04:48,719
Orion translated jeong into a Vietnamese word, tình, meaning affection, and sold it as a gift that carries your feelings.

49
00:04:48,999 --> 00:04:58,856
So in Korea, Choco Pie comforts homesick soldiers. In Vietnam, it's offered to great-grandparents. Not a bad career path for a cookie.

50
00:04:59,586 --> 00:05:02,845
In China, the company did something even smarter.

51
00:05:03,125 --> 00:05:10,988
Orion's Chinese name means good friend. And for Choco Pie, it swapped jeong for a different character,

52
00:05:10,988 --> 00:05:14,688
ren, a Confucian word for kindness and humanity.

53
00:05:14,968 --> 00:05:20,394
Same snack. Three countries. Three different words for love. And today,

54
00:05:20,394 --> 00:05:25,515
China is actually Orion's biggest market, bigger than Korea itself.

55
00:05:26,245 --> 00:05:30,878
Now we come to the strangest chapter. The one that got it banned.

56
00:05:31,608 --> 00:05:37,300
Just north of the border, there used to be a place called the Kaesong Industrial Complex.

57
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:46,760
South Korean companies ran factories there, and North Korean workers worked in them. It was one of the only places where the

58
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:49,129
two Koreas met every single day.

59
00:05:49,409 --> 00:05:54,391
The companies weren't allowed to hand out extra cash. So as a bonus,

60
00:05:54,391 --> 00:05:59,519
they gave workers snacks. And the snack everyone wanted was Choco Pie.

61
00:06:00,249 --> 00:06:04,760
Many workers didn't eat them. They took them home and sold them.

62
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:13,171
In twenty ten, a South Korean newspaper reported that a single Choco Pie could fetch as much as nine dollars and fifty cents on the

63
00:06:13,171 --> 00:06:14,785
North Korean black market.

64
00:06:15,065 --> 00:06:18,610
For a snack that costs a few cents in Seoul.

65
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:25,418
For a regime that controls almost everything people see and hear, that was a problem.

66
00:06:25,698 --> 00:06:31,704
Here was a sweet, cheap, delicious taste of the South, passed hand to hand across the country.

67
00:06:31,984 --> 00:06:36,275
Some called it a taste of capitalism in a plastic wrapper.

68
00:06:36,555 --> 00:06:44,015
So, according to reports in twenty fourteen, the factories were told to stop handing out Choco Pies,

69
00:06:44,015 --> 00:06:49,238
and give out things like sausages, instant noodles and coffee instead.

70
00:06:49,968 --> 00:06:52,382
And remember the balloons I promised you?

71
00:06:52,662 --> 00:06:59,587
That same year, South Korean activists tied ten thousand Choco Pies to giant helium balloons,

72
00:06:59,587 --> 00:07:02,193
and released them toward the North.

73
00:07:02,473 --> 00:07:08,792
A snack, floating over the most heavily guarded border in the world. You honestly can't make this up.

74
00:07:09,522 --> 00:07:11,034
So let's add it up.

75
00:07:11,314 --> 00:07:17,971
A hotel snack in Georgia inspired it. A Korean word for love sold it. Soldiers traded it,

76
00:07:17,971 --> 00:07:23,357
Russians fell for it, Vietnamese families offered it to their ancestors,

77
00:07:23,637 --> 00:07:27,675
and North Korea decided it was too dangerous to hand out.

78
00:07:28,405 --> 00:07:30,323
And here's the final twist.

79
00:07:30,603 --> 00:07:37,000
Today, about eighty-six percent of Choco Pie's sales come from outside Korea.

80
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:44,454
The snack Koreans think of as theirs has quietly become one of the most well-traveled cakes on the planet.

81
00:07:45,184 --> 00:07:52,782
So, where did you eat your first Choco Pie? Tell us in the comments. We're curious which country wins.

82
00:07:53,062 --> 00:07:59,888
If you like strange true stories about Korea, subscribe. There are a lot more where this came from.
